A presidential election was held in Portugal on 24 January 2016. The election chose the successor of President [[Aníbal Cavaco Silva]], who was [[Constitution of Portugal|constitutionally]] not allowed to run for a third consecutive term.

[[Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa]], the candidate supported by [[Social Democratic Party (Portugal)|Social Democratic Party]] (PSD), [[CDS – People's Party]] (CDS–PP) and [[People's Monarchist Party (Portugal)|People's Monarchist Party]] (PPM), won the election on the first round with 52% of the vote. Marcelo also won in every single district in the country and only lost a few [[Municipalities of Portugal|municipalities]] in the south of the country. The [[Socialist Party (Portugal)|Socialist Party]] (PS), for the first time in a Presidential election, did not officially support a candidate, with party members dividing their support for either [[António Sampaio da Nóvoa]] or [[Maria de Belém Roseira|Maria de Belém]],<ref>[https://rr.sapo.pt/noticia/presidenciais-2016/2016/01/20/belem-e-novoa-partem-o-ps-em-dois-saiba-quem-apoia-quem/44700/ Belém e Nóvoa partem o PS em dois. Saiba quem apoia quem], ''Renascença'', 20 January 2016. Retrieved 19 January 2025.</ref> which polled 23% and 4%, respectively. The Left Bloc's candidate, [[Marisa Matias]], polled a strong third place, with 10% of the votes, while the communist candidate, [[Edgar Silva]], achieved a very poor result, less than 4% of the votes. One of the big surprises in the election was the result of [[Eccentricity (behavior)|eccentric]] candidate [[Vitorino Silva]], polling at 3.3% of the votes, just 30,000 votes behind the communist candidate.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2016/01/25/politica/noticia/vitorino-silva-a-surpresa-que-veio-de-rans-1721284 |title=Vitorino Silva, a surpresa que veio de Rans |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=28 August 2026 |date=25 January 2016}}</ref>

For this election, about 9.7 million voters were registered to cast a ballot by election day.<ref name="AP"/> Turnout was higher than that of the [[2011 Portuguese presidential election|2011 election]], but reached a record low in a presidential election with a non incumbent, as only 48.66% of the electorate cast a ballot.<ref>[https://www.publico.pt/2016/01/25/politica/noticia/abstencao-em-x-e-a-maior-em-eleicoes-para-primeiro-mandato-na-presidencia-1721269 Abstenção de 51,16% é a maior em eleições para primeiro mandato na presidência ], Público (January 25, 2016).</ref> Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa was sworn in as President on 9 March 2016.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/tomada-de-posse-de-marcelo-rebelo-de-sousa-3/ |title=Tomada de posse de Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa |language=pt |work=RTP Arquivos |access-date=18 February 2026 |date=9 March 2016}}</ref>

==Background==
After being re-elected with 53% of the votes in January 2011, [[Aníbal Cavaco Silva]] had served two consecutive five-year terms as president, the [[Term limit|maximum number]], and the 2016 election was to choose a successor for a term beginning on 9 March. In Portugal, the president is the [[head of state]], has mostly ceremonial powers. However, the president does have some political influence and can [[Dissolution of Parliament|dissolve]] [[Assembly of the Republic (Portugal)|Parliament]] if a crisis occurs.<ref name="AP">[http://www.bigstory.ap.org/article/e9a8f54179d54d6194455a5ce8cf2412/10-candidates-run-portugals-presidential-election 10 candidates to run in Portugal's presidential election] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160206104421/http://www.bigstory.ap.org/article/e9a8f54179d54d6194455a5ce8cf2412/10-candidates-run-portugals-presidential-election |date=2016-02-06 }}, Associated Press (December 30, 2015).</ref><ref name="DW">[http://www.dw.com/en/portugal-presidential-election-likely-to-go-into-runoff/a-19001758 Portugal presidential election likely to go into runoff], Deutsche Welle (January 21, 2016).</ref> The president also has an [[official residence]] in the [[Belém Palace]] in Lisbon.<ref name="DW"/> Cavaco Silva's second term was dominated by the IMF/Eurozone bailout to rescue the country's finances and economy.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/politica/cavaco-termina-carreira-politica-historica-com-dois-mandatos-distintos_n901434 |title=Cavaco termina "carreira política histórica" com dois mandatos distintos |language=pt |work=RTP |access-date=26 August 2026| date=6 March 2016}}</ref> The complex economic situation forced the head of state to mediate talks between parties on several occasions,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://sol.iol.pt/2012/09/21/cavaco-considera-ultrapassada-hipotese-de-crise-politica |title=Cavaco considera 'ultrapassada' hipótese de crise política |language=pt |work=SOL |access-date=26 August 2026| date=21 September 2012}}</ref> one of them in 2013, after a government crisis in which Cavaco proposed a "unity government" between the PSD and PS to resolve the crisis, a proposal that was rejected by the parties.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2013/07/10/politica/noticia/cavaco-pede-governo-de-salvacao-nacional-e-aponta-eleicoes-para-junho-de-2014-1599887 |title=Cavaco exige acordo tripartido e eleições em Junho de 2014 |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=26 August 2026| date=10 July 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2013/07/21/politica/noticia/cavaco-defende-que-a-melhor-solucao-e-a-continuacao-do-governo-1600894 |title=Cavaco defende que "a melhor solução é a continuação do Governo" |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=26 August 2026| date=21 July 2013}}</ref> The dire economic picture, Cavaco's alignment with the then PSD/CDS–PP government and some controversial positions and gaffes,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2012/01/20/politica/noticia/cavaco-reforma-do-banco-de-portugal-nao-vai-chegar-para-pagar-despesas-1529976 |title=Cavaco diz que as reformas dele não chegarão para pagar despesas |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=27 August 2026| date=20 January 2012}}</ref> led to a [[Opinion polling on the Aníbal Cavaco Silva presidency#Second Term (2011–2016)|significant drop in his popularity ratings]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jornaldenegocios.pt/economia/politica/detalhe/porque_foi_tao_impopular_o_presidente_cavaco_silva |title=Porque foi tão impopular o Presidente Cavaco Silva? |language=pt |work=Jornal de Negócios |access-date=26 August 2026| date=8 March 2016}}</ref> For most of his second term, Cavaco Silva coexisted with PSD prime minister [[Pedro Passos Coelho]], with the institutional relationship being a good one,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://expresso.pt/politica/2018-10-24-Passos-e-as-revelacoes-de-Cavaco-Estou-a-escrever-sobre-os-mesmos-factos-o-ex-Presidente-nao-diz-tudo |title=Passos e as revelações de Cavaco: "Estou a escrever sobre os mesmos factos”, o ex-Presidente “não diz tudo” |language=pt |work=Expresso |access-date=26 August 2026| date=24 October 2018}}</ref> but after the [[2015 Portuguese legislative election|2015 legislative election]], which resulted in relative victory for Passos Coelho's coalition, but with a left-wing majority,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-34440667 |title=Portugal centre-right wins re-election despite bailout |language=en |work=[[BBC News]] |access-date=26 August 2026| date=5 October 2015}}</ref> the president had to cohabitate with Socialist prime minister [[António Costa]] for the last few months of his term.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://observador.pt/2015/11/23/10-temas-quentes-coabitacao-costa-cavaco/ |title=10 temas quentes para 4 meses de coabitação entre Costa e Cavaco |language=pt |work=Observador |access-date=26 August 2026| date=23 November 2015}}</ref> Cavaco Silva nominated Costa as prime minister after the left-wing majority rejected Passos Coelho's second minority government.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2015/11/24/politica/noticia/antonio-costa-indigitado-primeiroministro-por-cavaco-silva-1715392 |title=Cavaco nomeia Costa como primeiro-ministro |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=26 August 2026| date=24 November 2015}}</ref>

===Pre-campaign===
Just like in 2006 and 2011, the Socialist Party had no real strategy for the presidential election,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.dn.pt/pol%C3%ADtica/falta-de-estrat%C3%A9gia-ou-confus%C3%A3o-a-adapta%C3%A7%C3%A3o-do-ps-%C3%A0s-presidenciais |title=“Falta de estratégia” ou “confusão”: a adaptação do PS às presidenciais |language=pt |work=Diário de Notícias |access-date=26 August 2026| date=9 February 2025}}</ref> while on the PSD, the almost certain candidacy of [[Marcelo Rebelo de Sousa]] created doubts in the party's leadership, with party leader Pedro Passos Coelho expressing that the party's supported candidate could not be a "windmill", which was interpreted as a criticism towards Marcelo, despite a later denial from Passos Coelho.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.noticiasaominuto.com/politica/161113/passos-recusa-candidato-catavento-como-marcelo |title=Passos recusa candidato "catavento" como Marcelo |language=pt |work=Notícias ao Minuto|access-date=26 August 2026| date=19 January 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2016/01/13/politica/noticia/passos-coelho-nega-ter-chamado-catavento-a-marcelo-1720035 |title=Passos Coelho nega ter chamado catavento a Marcelo |language=pt |work=Público|access-date=26 August 2026| date=13 January 2016}}</ref> After a strong showing in the 2015 legislative election, the [[Left Bloc]] hoped to reinforce its position with the candidacy of [[Marisa Matias]]<ref name="publico2016">{{cite news |url=https://www.publico.pt/2016/01/25/politica/noticia/os-vencedores-1721283 |title=Vencedores e vencidos |language=pt |work=Público |access-date=26 August 2026| date=25 January 2016}}</ref> while the communists tried to recover from being overtaken by the Left Bloc, but presented a candidate from [[Madeira]], [[Edgar Silva]], with little national recognition.<ref name="publico2016" />

==Electoral system==
In order to stand for election, candidates must be of Portuguese origin and over 35 years old, gather 7,500 signatures of support one month before the election, and submit them to the [[Constitutional Court of Portugal]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://diariodarepublica.pt/dr/lexionario/termo/presidente-republica|title=Presidente da República |publisher=[[Diário da República]] |access-date=18 December 2024}}</ref>

Under Portuguese law, a candidate must receive a majority of votes (50% plus one vote) to be elected. If no candidate achieved a majority in the first round, a [[runoff election]] (i.e., second round, held between the two candidates who received the most votes in the first round) would have been held on February 14.<ref name="AP"/><ref name="DW"/>

==Candidates==
===Official candidates===
On 29 December 2015 the Constitutional Court certified a record ten candidates as having met the requirements to appear on the ballot. This remained the highest number of candidates in a Presidential ballot in Portuguese democracy until 2026.<ref name="AP"/>
